217 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 129 0 R/Pg 13 0 R/A[578 0 R 0]/K[6]>>
endobj

218 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 129 0 R/E(The Council is made up of 47 member states appointed by the General Assembly, based on equitable geographical distribution. A membership term is for three years and no member will be re-elected within two term limits. The Council is located in Geneva and is an intergovernmental body of the United Nations system, holding regular sessions all year round and special sessions when necessary.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

219 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 129 0 R/E( The Council works on a number of levels. The Universal Periodic Review is a process that reviews the human rights record of each UN member state on a rotating basis and allows each government to report on the steps it has taken to improve its human rights record. Special Procedures are independent experts and working groups that report on thematic issues or country specific situations that are connected with business practices, labour exploitation and extractive sector issues.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

220 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 129 0 R/T(Structure)/E(Structure)/Pg 13 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

221 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 129 0 R/E(The Council's main task is to implement universal respect for human rights and to examine instances and situations of violations of human rights, among others by making recommendations to the States. It can create commissions of inquiry or fact-finding missions when allegations of serious abuse occur, make resolutions urging states and other actors to correct their behavior, and, when gross and systematic violations have occurred, recommend that a member's participation in the Council be suspended to the General Assembly. While not binding like Security Council resolutions, its resolutions are nonetheless very normative and diplomatic and often have a great influence on further negotiation of treaties, national laws and corporate actions.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

222 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 129 0 R/T(Mandate and Powers)/E(Mandate and Powers)/Pg 13 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

223 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 129 0 R/E(The minerals upon which the modern global economy depends, such as the cobalt and copper in electric vehicle batteries, the gold in international reserves, and the tin, tantalum and tungsten in electronics manufacturing, are abundant in Africa. The majority of the world's mined cobalt comes from the Democratic Republic of the Congo and gold-producing belts cross the west and east of Africa. The global energy transition and growing use of digital technology have made these commodities more in demand than ever before.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

224 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 129 0 R/T(Introduction to the Agenda)/E(Introduction to the Agenda)/Pg 13 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

225 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 129 0 R/Pg 13 0 R/A[536 0 R 0]/K[14]>>
endobj

