<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 130 0 R/Pg 14 0 R/A[540 0 R 0]/K[5]>>
endobj

236 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 130 0 R/Pg 14 0 R/A[578 0 R 0]/K[6]>>
endobj

237 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 130 0 R/E( This expansion has not been paralleled by sufficient safeguards for workers exploiting these resources. Reporting of forced labour, debt bondage, child labour, work in hazardous conditions, wage theft, and violent repression of labour organizing persists in both artisanal and small scale and industrial mining, with credible reports by UN agencies, national labour ministries, academic researchers, and civil society groups. In 2025, the Rights Lab at the University of Nottingham reported that more than half of artisanal cobalt miners surveyed in the Democratic Republic of the Congo were victims of forced labour or severe exploitation, and in 2024, the U.S. Department of Labor \(DOL\) added cobalt from Congolese industrial mines to its forced labour designation, citing a growing body of evidence that such abuse is not limited to artisanal operations.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

238 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 130 0 R/E( Meanwhile, the supply chains of the minerals from Africa to world manufacturers are also hard to follow. Ore mined in undesirable circumstances often goes into products as a mix with legally-mined ore, rendering it hard to determine by downstream companies how their raw materials were mined. This agenda seeks to explore how and to what extent forced labour and exploitation occur in African mining, to review the effectiveness of current corporate accountability mechanisms, and to discuss the potential to enhance, without jeopardizing the livelihoods of the millions of Africans who rely on it, due diligence and supply chain transparency measures.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

239 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 582 0 R/E(Forced Labour: work or service obtained from a person by means of such penalty as to create a special pressure on him to do it and which he does not perform of his own free will, pursuant to the International Labour Organization's Forced Labour Convention \(No. 29\).)/Pg 14 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

240 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 583 0 R/E(Artisanal and Small-Scale Mining \(ASM\): low technology and labour-intensive mining and mineral processing mostly carried out by a person or a family or a small group of persons or cooperatives without much mechanisation or regulation.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

241 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 584 0 R/E(Large-Scale Mining \(LSM\): industrial mining by formally licensed companies with the aid of heavy machinery and extraction methods that require large capital investments, frequently in the form of joint ventures between multinational companies and state mining companies.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

242 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 585 0 R/E(Conflict Minerals: minerals mined in armed conflict or unstable situations that fund armed groups, typically referred to as tin, tantalum, tungsten and gold \(3TG\).)/Pg 14 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

