257 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 587 0 R/E(Artisanal and Small-Scale Mining \(ASM\): low technology and labour-intensive mining and mineral processing mostly carried out by a person or a family or a small group of persons or cooperatives without much mechanisation or regulation.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

258 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 588 0 R/E(Large-Scale Mining \(LSM\): industrial mining by formally licensed companies with the aid of heavy machinery and extraction methods that require large capital investments, frequently in the form of joint ventures between multinational companies and state mining companies.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

259 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 589 0 R/E(Conflict Minerals: minerals mined in armed conflict or unstable situations that fund armed groups, typically referred to as tin, tantalum, tungsten and gold \(3TG\).)/Pg 15 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

260 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 590 0 R/E(Due Diligence: the process through which a company identifies, prevents, mitigates and discloses its approach to human rights and environmental impacts that are adverse and can arise in its operations and supply chain.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

261 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 591 0 R/E(Supply Chain Traceability: ability to trace a mineral from its extraction all the way through smelting, refining and manufacturing until it is delivered to the end product, allowing for verification of sourcing conditions.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

262 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 592 0 R/E(Debt Bondage: a type of forced labour where a worker is forced to work for a pit owner or trader to pay off a debt, typically with conditions that ensure that repayment is virtually impossible, such as in the case of advance payment between miners and traders or pit owners.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

263 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 131 0 R/T(Overview of the African Mining Sector)/E(Overview of the African Mining Sector)/Pg 15 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

264 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 131 0 R/T(The Critical Minerals Boom)/E(The Critical Minerals Boom)/Pg 15 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

265 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 131 0 R/E(A large portion of several new minerals that are key to the global energy transition are found in Africa. The Democratic Republic of the Congo is the world's largest producer of mined cobalt \(which is used in lithium-ion batteries\), with the Lualaba and Haut-Katanga provinces producing about 75% of all mined cobalt in the world in 2024. )/Pg 15 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

266 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 131 0 R/Pg 15 0 R/A[536 0 R 0]/K[17]>>
endobj

