endobj

277 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 132 0 R/Pg 16 0 R/A[578 0 R 0]/K[6]>>
endobj

278 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 132 0 R/E(South Africa is the largest producer of platinum group metals, Guinea has one of the world's largest bauxite deposits and gold is mined across a swath of West Africa from Ghana to Mali, Burkina Faso, C\364te d'Ivoire, Guinea and Senegal as well as in Sudan, South Sudan and Tanzania.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

279 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 132 0 R/E( With the growth of electric vehicle production and renewable energy storage, the demand for battery metals has grown in the global market, increasing the investment in cobalt from African countries such as DRC, especially by Chinese State-backed and private companies who now account for a significant portion of cobalt production in the country and an even larger downstream refining capacity. This investment has created jobs and infrastructure as well as export revenue for the producing countries, though regulation has not always been able to keep up with the rate of growth and unpolicied gaps are enabling labour exploitation.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

280 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 132 0 R/T(Artisanal, Small-Scale and Industrial Mining)/E(Artisanal, Small-Scale and Industrial Mining)/Pg 16 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

281 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 132 0 R/E(Two general systems of mineral extraction are in use on the continent, often working concurrently. Licensed industrial mining is carried out by companies, frequently multinational joint ventures with state mining companies, employing formalization and mechanized methods. Artisanal and small-scale mining is, on the other hand, informal, labour intensive and mostly non-mechanised, using hand picks, shovels and pans to dig, separate and wash ore. Well in excess of 200,000 Congolese would be involved in artisanal cobalt mining if estimates are correct, while millions of people in West and East Africa are involved in artisanal gold mining.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

282 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 132 0 R/E( The artisanal sector is informal, and this is at the heart of the problem of forced labour. They are often not hired with a contract, don't receive any safety gear, don't have minimum wage protections and are often subject to debt bondage with pit owners or traders by receiving advance payments. However, investigations have revealed that there is a parallel lack of accountability at the large-scale end of the sector as workers are engaged in subcontract arrangements, many of which are not directly monitored by the industrial company.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

283 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 132 0 R/Pg 16 0 R/A[536 0 R 0]/K[12]>>
endobj

284 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 132 0 R/Pg 16 0 R/A[581 0 R 0]/K[13]>>
endobj

285 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 132 0 R/Pg 16 0 R/A[573 0 R 0]/K[14]>>
endobj

286 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 132 0 R/Pg 16 0 R/A[574 0 R 0]/K[15]>>
endobj

287 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 132 0 R/Pg 16 0 R/A[537 0 R 0]/K[16]>>
endobj

288 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 133 0 R/Pg 17 0 R/A[580 0 R 0]/K[0]>>
endobj

289 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 133 0 R/Pg 17 0 R/A[577 0 R 0]/K[1]>>
endobj

290 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(mun@umt.edu.pk)/Pg 17 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

291 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(umt_mun)/Pg 17 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

292 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 133 0 R/Pg 17 0 R/A[541 0 R 0]/K[4]>>
endobj

293 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 133 0 R/Pg 17 0 R/A[540 0 R 0]/K[5]>>
endobj

294 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 133 0 R/Pg 17 0 R/A[578 0 R 0]/K[6]>>
endobj

295 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 133 0 R/T(Forms of Exploitation)/E(Forms of Exploitation)/Pg 17 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

296 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(African mining has many overlapping forms of exploitation. In artisanal operations, miners are frequently provisioned by the pit financier with food, tools or cash, and then expected to pay back a fixed percentage of their haul, sometimes at the financier's own price, until the debt is paid off. Even when wages exist, they are often well below a living wage: In the Kolwezi cobalt belt, a 2025 field study revealed that many industrial workers in the sector who worked physically intensive and dangerous jobs were paid almost two dollars per day, when wages were paid at all.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

297 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E( In both artisanal and industrial workplaces there are significant occupational health and safety issues. Unregulated artisanal pits often experience tunnel collapses, protective equipment is often lacking, and workers and communities around the artisanal pits are exposed to processing chemicals \(such as mercury and cyanide\) and heavy metals for long-term effects. In 2025, water samples were collected near cobalt mines and found to be contaminated with high levels of water pollutants, well beyond the level recommended by the WHO. There are also regular restrictions on freedom of association, such as restrictions on union activity and access to grievance systems for subcontracted workers, reported in the reporting period.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

298 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 133 0 R/T(Patterns of Forced Labour and Exploitation)/E(Patterns of Forced Labour and Exploitation)/Pg 17 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

299 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 133 0 R/T(Child Labour in the Mining Sector)/E(Child Labour in the Mining Sector)/Pg 17 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

300 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 133 0 R/E(Artisanal mining continues to be a stoking issue on the continent involving child labour. It is estimated from reports that there are tens of thousands of children under the age of 18 or even as young as six, engaged in artisanal cobalt mining in Congo, performing various activities including digging, sorting and transporting, in conditions that expose them to the risk of injury and toxic exposure. In West Africa's gold belt, the International Labour Organization and Human Rights Watch have cited the presence of tens of thousands of children in West Africa's gold belt working in artisanal gold mining, often in direct contact with mercury used in gold amalgamation, in Mali alone. Children's employment in artisanal mining is a constant despite the national and international ban on child labour, due to the poverty conditions in which mining takes place, the lack of access to formal education, and the nature of the mining work, which is predominantly formal and cash-based.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

301 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 133 0 R/Pg 17 0 R/A[536 0 R 0]/K[13]>>
endobj

302 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 133 0 R/Pg 17 0 R/A[581 0 R 0]/K[14]>>
endobj

303 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 133 0 R/Pg 17 0 R/A[573 0 R 0]/K[15]>>
endobj

