<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 134 0 R/Pg 18 0 R/A[578 0 R 0]/K[6]>>
endobj

313 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 134 0 R/T(The Democratic Republic of the Congo: Cobalt and Copper)/E(The Democratic Republic of the Congo: Cobalt and Copper)/Pg 18 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

314 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(The cobalt belt in the DRC is the best documented case of forced labor in African mining. In a study of over 1,400 artisanal miners conducted by the University of Nottingham Rights Lab in Lualaba and Haut-Katanga, more than a third said they had suffered forced labour and a majority said they had been subjected to conditions that met a wider definition of severe exploitation. The United States Department of Labor \(DOL\) has added cobalt from Congolese industrial mines to its List of Goods Produced by Child or Forced Labour, following investigations that revealed subcontracted labour abuses at several large facilities, including joint ventures by Glencore, the Eurasian Resources Group and Chinese state-backed mining companies, in September 2024.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

315 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E( This added to the fact that about 70 percent of the DRC's cobalt is exported to China through Chinese-owned or Chinese-financed mines, and a roughly 90 percent share of Congolese cobalt is processed in China, makes it difficult to determine who is responsible for what's going on at the point of extraction. Civil society organizations in the region, including RAID and AFREWATCH, report some positive developments since their groundbreaking 2021 report, including new collective bargaining agreements at a few Chinese-owned mines, and an increase in the number of labour inspectors deployed. But they note that conditions for subcontracted workers are still harsh and that the majority still do not earn a living wage.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

316 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 134 0 R/T(Regional Case Studies)/E(Regional Case Studies)/Pg 18 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

317 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 134 0 R/T(West Africa's Gold Belt)/E(West Africa's Gold Belt)/Pg 18 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

318 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 134 0 R/E(Artisanal and small-scale mining are a significant part of the gold industry in Ghana, Mali, Burkina Faso and neighboring countries. In Mali, the government's research and Human Rights Watch's documentation have revealed that a significant portion of the artisanal mining sector also consists of children, with many involved in gold amalgamation exposed to mercury, and there are also documented cases of adult forced labour. In Ghana, where galamsey \(informal mining\) is a major problem, it has grown rapidly in recent years, leading to child labour, hazardous working environments, environmental damage and has become a national political hot topic, because of the impact it has on export revenues and GDP. The U.S. government has found evidence or the risk of forced labour in gold mining in over 12 African states, indicating that the issue is not limited to any one country.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

