331 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 135 0 R/T(Conflict Minerals and Armed Groups)/E(Conflict Minerals and Armed Groups)/Pg 19 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

332 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 135 0 R/E(Some areas of the eastern DRC and the Sahel are experiencing direct linkages between mineral extraction and armed conflict. Historically, profits from gold, tin, tantalum and tungsten have funded non-state armed groups in the eastern DRC, and in Sudan, the United Nations has recorded connections between gold mining and smuggling networks and the funding of the conflict, in addition to the ongoing use of child recruitment by non-State Armed Groups. Where mining operations are in the hands of armed groups, workers are particularly vulnerable to coercion and international initiatives to trace and limit the trade in conflict minerals are being created specifically to address this situation, including the 3TG designation used in the EU and US regulations.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

333 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 135 0 R/T(Legal and Regulatory Frameworks)/E(Legal and Regulatory Frameworks)/Pg 19 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

334 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 135 0 R/T(International Labour Standards)/E(International Labour Standards)/Pg 19 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

335 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 135 0 R/E(The ILO is the body which establishes the main global standards regarding forced and child labour. The Forced Labour Convention \(No. 29\) of 1930 and its 2014 Protocol call on states to eliminate forced labour and provide remedy for victims, the Worst Forms of Child Labour Convention \(No. 182\) of 1999 obligates states to eliminate hazardous child labour, which includes mining, and the Minimum Age Convention \(No. 138\) establishes minimum age limits for work. All the member states of the ILO have agreed to respect and promote these principles in the 1998 Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work, irrespective of whether they are ratified.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

336 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 135 0 R/T(The UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights)/E(The UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights)/Pg 19 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

337 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 135 0 R/E(In 2011, the UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights were endorsed by the UN Human Rights Council, which require States to take steps to protect human rights from the actions of third parties, including companies and companies to take steps to respect human rights in their operations and value chains, including through human rights due diligence. While the Guiding Principles are the key international benchmark for corporate accountability in the extractive sector, they are not binding and can only be effective in practice with implementation in the national context and voluntary adoption by companies.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

338 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 135 0 R/Pg 19 0 R/A[536 0 R 0]/K[14]>>
endobj

