350 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 136 0 R/T(The OECD Due Diligence Guidance)/E(The OECD Due Diligence Guidance)/Pg 20 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

351 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 136 0 R/E(The OECD Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible Supply Chains of Minerals from Conflict-Affected and High-Risk Areas outlines five steps that companies are expected to take: setting up effective management systems, identifying and assessing supply chain risk, designing and implementing a response strategy to the identified risks, arranging independent third-party audits of smelter and refinery due diligence practices, and publicly reporting on due diligence performance. It is mineral neutral, and has been most fully translated into practice for the minerals of the DMTGs, through industry projects like the Responsible Minerals Initiative.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

352 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 136 0 R/T(Corporate Accountability)/E(Corporate Accountability)/Pg 20 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

353 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 136 0 R/T(Regional and National Measures)/E(Regional and National Measures)/Pg 20 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

354 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 136 0 R/E(The EU Conflict Minerals Regulation which came into effect in 2021, is making EU importers of tin, tantalum, tungsten and gold with above threshold volumes implement OECD conflict mineral supply chain due diligence. The general \215Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence Directive\216 adopted in 2024 also broadened the scope of the requirements for human rights and environmental due diligence for large companies with EU operations, but with the time limits for implementation having been postponed in the latest proposals from the Commission in 2025. The Department of Labor in the United States maintains the List of Goods Produced by Child Labor or Forced Labor, including cobalt and gold from several African countries, and the U.S. customs authorities have the power to issue Withhold Release Orders prohibiting goods with reasonable suspicions of being tainted with forced labour. The Modern Slavery Act \(MSA\) 2018 mandates that large businesses make statements about their efforts to prevent forced labour and trafficking within their supply chains once a year.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

355 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 136 0 R/T(The Role of Mining Companies)/E(The Role of Mining Companies)/Pg 20 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

356 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 136 0 R/E(As either direct operators or partners in joint ventures with state mining enterprises, companies are primarily responsible for conditions at their own mines and from the practices of subcontractors they utilize. There have been investigations of various large copper and cobalt mines in the DRC, and it has been established that the abuse is limited to subcontracted workers, leaving the lead operators at a safe distance from the site, even when conditions at the mining site are far lower than international standards. In some instances, companies have reacted to criticism by dismissing subcontractors who have been abusing workers and by implementing social auditing and raising wages in certain operations, but civil society groups say that such \215good faith\216 corporate actions have not been consistently enforced or independently verified throughout the industry.)/Pg 20 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

357 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 136 0 R/Pg 20 0 R/A[536 0 R 0]/K[14]>>
endobj

358 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 136 0 R/Pg 20 0 R/A[581 0 R 0]/K[15]>>
