369 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 137 0 R/T(Downstream Manufacturers and End-Users)/E(Downstream Manufacturers and End-Users)/Pg 21 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

370 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 137 0 R/E(In addition to the mining companies, it also applies to the smelters, refiners, battery and electronics producers and the brands that make use of the minerals in their products. The source of raw material from several mines and sourcing conditions is often mixed together prior to or during the refining process, making it technically challenging to trace a particular batch of cobalt, gold or copper from the mine and claim to have some visibility of conditions further upstream. The aim of emerging value chain due diligence laws is to fill this gap by imposing legal liabilities on large companies to trace and monitor their entire value chain, instead of relying solely on suppliers' self-assessments.)/Pg 21 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

371 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 137 0 R/T(Towards Responsible Mineral Supply Chains)/E(Towards Responsible Mineral Supply Chains)/Pg 21 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

372 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 137 0 R/E(Several multi-stakeholder programs make efforts to make due diligence guidance work in terms of operational traceability systems. The Responsible Minerals Initiative has audit and assessment programs for smelters and refiners of the four target minerals \(tin, tantalum, tungsten, and gold\) that enable downstream companies to track their supply chain to identify companies that comply with the recognized due diligence principles. The International Tin Supply Chain Initiative \(ITSCI\) is a chain-of-custody system for bag-and-tag traceability that is used in the mines of portions of Central Africa to help trace minerals through export. The Kimberley Process Certification Scheme, although limited and only focusing on rough diamonds, is a useful example of how a mineral-specific certification scheme could be designed, and a reminder of the challenges faced by the type of certification regimes which address only the financing of armed conflict and not the wider scope of labour rights violations.)/Pg 21 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

373 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 137 0 R/E( These mechanisms continue to be hampered. Implementing and monitoring a certification and traceability system is expensive, and the poorest artisanal miners may be excluded from the formal market without helping to solve the root causes of poverty that are responsible for attracting people to risky and low-paying mining operations. Another criticism is that historically, due diligence processes have been more concentrated on conflict financing than on labour conditions, allowing a mineral to meet due diligence requirements regarding armed groups' involvement in the mines, whilst still being extracted using forced or child labour. Supply chain traceability coupled with direct investments in the formalization of artisanal mining, fair pricing arrangements for the small-scale miners and a greater capacity for labour inspection in the producing states would be essential for effective reform.)/Pg 21 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

374 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 137 0 R/Pg 21 0 R/A[536 0 R 0]/K[12]>>
endobj

375 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 137 0 R/Pg 21 0 R/A[581 0 R 0]/K[13]>>
