386 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 138 0 R/T(International Response)/E(International Response)/Pg 22 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

387 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 138 0 R/E(In recent years, the attention paid to labour conditions in African mining has significantly increased due to the large volumes of cobalt and battery metal requirements linked to the energy transition. UN Special Procedures, such as the Working Group on Business and Human Rights, have discussed the extractive sector as part of their mandate on corporate accountability, while the UN human rights mechanisms have periodically discussed concerns regarding mining-linked abuses during UPRs for producing States. The ILO goes on with technical cooperation programs to combat child labour in artisanal mining operations in West and Central Africa.)/Pg 22 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

388 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 138 0 R/E( National and regional regulators have walked on parallel paths. The US has expanded its forced labour designations to include both artisanal and industrial cobalt from the DRC and gold from over a dozen African states has documented evidence or identified a risk of forced labour, according to the US Trafficking in Persons Report. The European Union has introduced mandatory due diligence obligations for 3TG minerals, and is proposing to extend the scope of its human rights due diligence \(CSDD\) to large companies that operate in the EU market, despite recent changes to the timeline for implementation. Producer\220s states, including the DRC and Ghana, have implemented plans to enhance the capacity of labour inspection and formalize artisanal mining, but there is a lack of resources to enforce these plans and in some areas, insecurity persists.)/Pg 22 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

389 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 138 0 R/T(Implications)/E(Implications)/Pg 22 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

390 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 138 0 R/E(The human toll of forced labour and exploitation in mining is felt by the individual miners and their families, including injury, chronic illnesses resulting from toxic conditions at mining sites, the loss of education for children working in the mines and the perpetuation of poverty due to debt bondage and low wages. The consequences of mining are not restricted to miners, but extend to communities that surround the mines as well, with consequences like water pollution and land degradation.)/Pg 22 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

391 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 138 0 R/T(Human and Social Costs)/E(Human and Social Costs)/Pg 22 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

392 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 138 0 R/E(In producing states, mining exports also form a significant component of GDP and foreign exchange earnings, and hence, any national and international interests in the labour conditions are far from trivial. )/Pg 22 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

393 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 138 0 R/T(Economic and Trade Consequences)/E(Economic and Trade Consequences)/Pg 22 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

